---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

import { SOURCES, SOURCE_GROUPS } from '../data/sources';
---

<Base
  title="About & method — Human in the AI Era"
  description="How this site argues, what it relies on, what it is uncertain about, and the sources behind every claim."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="ahead">
      <p class="label">Colophon</p>
      <h1>About &amp; method</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="body">
      <h2>What this is</h2>
      <p>
        A working argument about how human beings live well when thinking,
        reasoning and cognitive labour are increasingly performed by machines.
        Fourteen essays, a delegation framework, and a self-assessment. It is not
        a news site and it is not futurism — it does not forecast capability, and
        nothing in it depends on a particular timeline being right.
      </p>

      <h2>How it argues</h2>
      <p>Every essay follows the same discipline:</p>
      <ol class="method">
        <li><strong>State a claim</strong> that could be false, in one paragraph, marked as such.</li>
        <li><strong>Give the evidence</strong> it rests on, named, with its weaknesses stated where they exist.</li>
        <li><strong>State the strongest objection</strong> as its best proponent would state it — not a convenient version.</li>
        <li><strong>Answer it, or concede.</strong> Several pages concede. <a class="link" href="/topics/steelman">One entire page</a> is objections.</li>
        <li><strong>End with practices</strong> that are specific enough to do on a Tuesday.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What it is uncertain about</h2>
      <p>
        Stated plainly, because a site arguing for calibration should be
        calibrated. We do not know whether this technology differs in kind from
        writing, printing and the calculator, or whether that belief is what
        every generation holds about its own tools — the base rate for this
        genre of alarm is poor, and we could be part of it. We do not know
        whether AI companionship will bridge people toward human contact or
        away from it. We do not know how much of the graduate wage premium is
        signalling. Where the evidence is correlational or contested, the pages
        say so rather than rounding up.
      </p>

      <h2>Design</h2>
      <p>
        The two-colour system is load-bearing rather than decorative. Teal marks
        the machine end of every scale and sienna marks the human end; the
        <a class="link" href="/ladder">Delegation Ladder</a> interpolates between
        them, and the same axis runs through the audit results. Set in Fraunces,
        Newsreader and IBM Plex Mono. Built with Astro, no client-side framework;
        the only JavaScript on the site is the theme toggle, the reading
        progress bar, and the audit's scoring — which runs locally and transmits
        nothing.
      </p>

      <h2>Contributing</h2>
      <p>
        The source is on
        <a class="link" href="https://github.com/jdmunta/human-in-the-ai-era" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
        The most valuable contribution is a better objection: if you can break an
        argument here, open an issue and it goes on the
        <a class="link" href="/topics/steelman">steelman page</a> with attribution.
        Corrections to sources are equally welcome.
      </p>

      <h2>Revision history</h2>
      <p>
        Calling something a working argument is easy; being able to check it is
        the point. The <a class="link" href="/changelog">changelog</a> is
        generated from the repository's commit history, so it shows when each
        essay was written, when it was last revised, and how many times — not a
        hand-kept list that can quietly drift from what happened.
      </p>

      <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Everything the essays rely on, by area. Where a figure is famous but
        contested, the caveat is noted here as well as in the text.
      </p>

      {
        SOURCE_GROUPS.map((g) => (
          <section class="srcgroup">
            <p class="label">{g}</p>
            <ul>
              {SOURCES.filter((s) => s.group === g).map((s) => (
                <li>
                  <span set:html={s.cite} />
                  {s.caveat && <span class="caveat">{s.caveat}</span>}
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </section>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .ahead { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem) 1.5rem; }
  .ahead h1 { font-size: var(--t-h1); margin-top: 1rem; }
  .body { max-width: var(--measure); padding-bottom: 5rem; }
  .body h2 {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: var(--t-h2);
    margin-block: 2.8rem 0.9rem;
    max-width: 22ch;
  }
  .body h2::before {
    content: '';
    display: block;
    width: 2.4rem; height: 2px;
    background: var(--human);
    margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
  }
  .body > p { margin-bottom: 1.1em; }
  .method { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 1.2rem 0 0; counter-reset: m; }
  .method li {
    counter-increment: m;
    position: relative;
    padding: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 2.4rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .method li:first-child { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .method li strong { color: var(--ink); }
  .method li::before {
    content: counter(m, decimal-leading-zero);
    position: absolute; left: 0; top: 1.05rem;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    color: var(--human);
  }
  .srcgroup { margin-top: 2rem; }
  .srcgroup .label { color: var(--human); display: block; margin-bottom: 0.8rem; }
  .srcgroup ul { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
  .srcgroup li {
    padding: 0.6rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .srcgroup li:first-child { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .srcgroup :global(.caveat) {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    color: var(--machine);
    margin-top: 0.3rem;
  }
</style>
